title: 'pallet-revive: cold/hot pricing for calls, transfers, code loads and contract creation'
doc:
- audience: [Runtime Dev, Runtime User]
  description: |-
    The cold/hot pricing introduced in #12104 now also covers the state items accessed by the call
    opcodes (EVM `CALL`, `STATICCALL`, `DELEGATECALL` and the PVM call and delegate-call host
    functions), a call's value transfer, code loads and contract creation, following EIP-2929. An
    item is hot if it was accessed earlier in the same transaction. Each operation is charged the
    cold benchmark if any of the items it accesses is cold, and the hot benchmark if all are hot.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `WeightInfo` has 12 new required methods: `code_load`, `account_info_read`, `seal_call_hot`,
    `seal_call_transfer_hot`, `seal_delegate_call_hot`, `call_with_pvm_code_per_byte_hot`,
    `call_with_evm_code_per_byte_hot`, `access_list_touch_hot_upgrade`,
    `access_list_touch_cold_address_empty`, `access_list_touch_cold_address_full`,
    `access_list_touch_hot_address_single_element` and `access_list_touch_hot_address_full`.
    Some existing benchmarks also measure different work now, so runtimes with their own
    `pallet_revive` weights must regenerate them:
    - `call`, `eth_call`, `seal_call`, `seal_delegate_call`, `instantiate` and `seal_instantiate`
      no longer measure the code reads, which `code_load` charges separately. Their weights drop,
      but a cold call or instantiation now also pays `code_load`, so its total weight probably
      grows rather than drops.
    - `seal_instantiate` and `evm_instantiate` also measure a read of the new address's
      `OriginalAccount` entry.
    - `access_list_touch_hot_full` now touches a read-paid entry with a read, so it no longer
      measures an upgrade; `access_list_touch_hot_upgrade` measures that.
    - The benchmarks that fill the access list now fill it to 4,096 entries.

    Code outside the pallet that uses `pallet_revive::precompiles`:
    - `Ext` no longer has `touch_storage_access` and `peek_storage_access`, and
      `StorageAccessKind::Persistent` no longer holds a plain `Warmth`, so `StorageAccessKind::new`
      no longer takes one either. Code that charged a storage access should charge the cold price
      with the new `StorageAccessKind::persistent_cold`, or use `StorageAccessKind::Transient` for
      transient storage, for example:
      ```rust
      env.frame_meter_mut().charge_weight_token(RuntimeCosts::GetStorage {
          len,
          kind: StorageAccessKind::persistent_cold(),
      })?;
      ```
    - In `RuntimeCosts`, `DelegateCallBase` is removed, `CallBase` takes the warmth of the call's
      entries as a new parameter, `CallTransferSurcharge` takes a `TransferAccessKind` in place of
      `dust_transfer`, and `AccountInfoRead` and `AccessListRegistration` are new. Code that
      charged `CallBase`, `DelegateCallBase` or `CallTransferSurcharge` can no longer build these
      variants; it should charge the cold price from `WeightInfo` instead, for example
      `env.charge(T::WeightInfo::seal_call(transfer, dust, 0))`, or `seal_delegate_call()` for a
      delegate call.

    `ContractBlob::store_code` now returns `Option<BalanceOf<T>>`: the deposit collected for newly
    stored code, or `None` when the code was already stored.

    The access list holds up to 4,096 entries per transaction (was 2,048). The memory budgeted
    for its worst case, which `integrity_test` counts against `RuntimeMemory`, grows from 1.5 MiB
    to 2.5 MiB, so a runtime close to its memory limit can now fail that check. `integrity_test`
    now also checks that the normal-class block proof limit allows at most 1,024 cold storage
    slots per transaction, unless that limit is above `PVFMemory`.

    No storage migration is needed.
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    A call or delegate call to an EIP-7702 delegated account whose delegation target is not a
    deployed contract now also pays for reading the target's account info, which tells whether
    the target is itself delegated.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch
